<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kerma</h1><p class="st-meta">2500 BCE – 1500 BCE (approx.) · capital Kerma</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first great kingdom of Nubia, centred on Kerma at the Third Cataract and for centuries a rival to pharaonic Egypt.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kerma culture developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-nubia">Upper Nubia</a> from about 2500 BCE, with its centre at Kerma near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-cataracts">Third Cataract</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. The town grew from a farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-herding settlement into the capital of a kingdom that Egyptian texts called Kush, controlling the river trade in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a> and cattle.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rulers lived in a walled city dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-deffufa">Western Deffufa</a>, a massive mud-brick temple. Kings were buried under huge circular tumuli, some with hundreds of sacrificed attendants. Kerma potters made distinctive polished black-topped red ware. At its height, in the 17th century BCE, Kerma raided as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elkab">Elkab</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/fifteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Hyksos</a> of the Fifteenth Dynasty of Egypt sought its alliance against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> struck back. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a> sacked Kerma around 1500 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viceroyalty-of-kush">Viceroyalty of Kush</a>. Kerma traditions survived beneath Egyptian rule and fed into the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire of Napata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kerma.jpg" alt="Kerma" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Lassi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerma_city.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2500 BCE – 1500 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kerma</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nedjeh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Kerma, Kingdom of Kush (Kerma), Yam</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2000&amp;focus=kerma" aria-label="Kerma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5501 1452 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kerma lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5821 1708L5862 1746M5821 1708L5863 1489M5821 1708L5856 1595"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5862 1746h0M5863 1489h0M5856 1595h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5821 1708h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5821 1708h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-2000&amp;focus=kerma">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
